    "quantization_distribution": {
      "why_binary_underperforms_here": "Measured on 2,000 Cohere embed-v4 vectors. The published Cohere failure mode attributes poor binary quantization to dimensions concentrated near zero without balanced sign. That specific mechanism is NOT present in embed-v4: global balance is 0.5022 and only 5.4% of components sit within 10% of zero. The cause here is per-dimension sign constancy instead: 448 of 1024 dimensions are more than 80% one-sided, several are entirely one-sided, so roughly 44% of the bits carry no discriminative information. What survives packs every vector into a 286-398 hamming band out of 1024 bits, and hamming ordering agrees with cosine ordering on only 20 of the top 50.",
      "fraction_components_positive": 0.5022,
      "components_within_10pct_of_zero": 0.054,
      "dimensions_over_80pct_one_sided": 448,
      "dimensions_total": 1024,
      "hamming_band": [
        286,
        398
      ],
      "top50_hamming_cosine_overlap": 0.4,
      "distinct_bit_patterns": "2000 of 2000 sampled"
    },
    "blog_operating_point": {
      "correction": "An earlier reading of these representations concluded binary underperforms on this corpus. That was an artifact of measuring at ef_search=100 with rerank depth <=200. At the deep-rerank operating point (ef_search=800, rerank 1400) binary reaches 0.9967 and EXCEEDS fp32 HNSW's 0.9933, because reranking 1,400 candidates by exact cosine surfaces neighbours that fp32 HNSW's greedy ef_search=100 traversal never visits. Recall saturates at 1400; 3000 adds nothing.",
      "reference": "AWS Blog (forthcoming)",
      "anchors": 30,
      "k": 10,
