      ],
      "tradeoff": "At 500K the fp32 index fits in memory, so fp32 stays cheaper: 2.00 ms and 1,914 buffers against 22.36 ms and 26,704. That ordering inverts once the fp32 index exceeds memory, which is the regime binary quantization exists for."
    },
    "native_binary_comparison": {
      "question": "Do the embeddings need regenerating to make binary quantization work?",
      "answer": "No, and it is provable three ways.",
      "evidence": [
        "The stored vectors are already unit length: L2 norm 1.000000 across the sample, so the published advice to normalise before quantizing is already satisfied.",
        "Normalising cannot change a single bit. binary_quantize thresholds at zero and positive scaling is sign-preserving, verified by construction.",
        "Cohere embed-v4 can return a native `binary` embedding type from Bedrock (128 int8 = 1024 bits). Measured against the same fp32 ground truth on a 3,000-product pool it is indistinguishable from binary_quantize: 572 of 1024 informative bits in both, 5 fully constant in both, and overfetch curves within noise at every depth."
      ],
      "api_reproducibility": {
